            <ArticleTitle>Rasch analysis of the Italian version of fear avoidance beliefs questionnaire (FABQ-I).</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>151-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/09638288.2014.909890</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To perform a Rasch analysis (RA) of the Fear Avoidance Beliefs questionnaire (FABQ-I) in a sample of chronic non-specific low back pain (CLBP) patients. The RA is expected to provide information about the internal construct validity and all the single items consistency of the FABQ-I and then contributing to the knowledge about the questionnaire's clinimetric properties.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred and fifty subjects with CLBP were enrolled into the study. The FABQ-I have been studied by means of RA. The sample size needed to obtain stable person, item and rating-scale calibrations for use with the Rasch model was based on the level of error expected in the measure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">RA did not support the total scale as an unidimensional measure of Fear Avodiance Beliefs. Also the analysis of the Physical Activity (PA) and Word (WO) subscales as well as the attempts of manipulation failed in achieving an acceptable fit to the Rasch model.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">FABQ-I may be considered to reflect a multiple psychological constructs describing scale. The raw score of the FABQ-I and changes in scores must be interpreted with caution because as a general measure of fear avoid beliefs was not supported.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
